Z||000000||Voytek||04-18-2001||11:05 AM||||Ok,<BR>Blonde? - definitely<BR>Young? - well....not old, anyways<BR>Hot? - jury's still out on that one<BR>But I do have a question and would appreciate any comments:<BR>Does anyone's Fiero seem to be really succeptable to humidity changes?  When I say succeptable, I mean substantial differences in power.  I have noticed that my '87 GT seems to have a LOT more juice when the days are cloudy, wet and humid (70-80%) and seems sluggish (by comparison) when the days are dry and windy (20-50% humidity).  I know more oxygen means more power, but should it be THIS succeptable to changes in weather?  Could this be related to oxygen sensor (or any other sensor for that matter)??  Thanks for the input.||||reg||7
Z||000001||Kerri 98||04-18-2001||11:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B>Ok,<BR>Blonde? - definitely<BR>Young? - well....not old, anyways<BR>Hot? - jury's still out on that one<BR>But I do have a question and would appreciate any comments:<BR>Does anyone's Fiero seem to be really succeptable to humidity changes?  When I say succeptable, I mean substantial differences in power.  I have noticed that my '87 GT seems to have a LOT more juice when the days are cloudy, wet and humid (70-80%) and seems sluggish (by comparison) when the days are dry and windy (20-50% humidity).  I know more oxygen means more power, but should it be THIS succeptable to changes in weather?  Could this be related to oxygen sensor (or any other sensor for that matter)??  Thanks for the input.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Have no answer for you but may be able to help the jury decide. Post a pic , HAHA||||reg||7
Z||000002||Taijiguy||04-18-2001||11:20 AM||||That's pretty typical actually. Cool cloudy, even foggy days, the air is more dense. The amount that's pulled in per stroke is the same, but since there is so much water in the air, and water doesn't really compress, it's almost like having a higher compression ratio. Plus, like I mentioned in another post about water injection, when you get combustion, the water turns to steam, which expands faster and more absolutely than gases, and gives a slower, cooler, more complete burn. That's why there seems to be more and better power on those cold wet days. <p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 04-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||04-18-2001||11:20 AM||||from the desk of a hot, superstud:<P> yes humidity and cool temps make more horsepower. colder air is more dense, the moisture (humidity) makes the air cooler. Lots of WWII fighter aircraft used water injection to boost horsepower. Thats been done for cars too. All thats needed is a container for the water, a small fitting into the intake. similar to NOS system. Race cars have to allow for differences from afternoon qualifying to nite time racing.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||04-18-2001||11:23 AM||||If it was the other way around, if it ran poorly when it was humid or rainy I would suspect your spark plug wires or rotor cap were shot.<P>  Maybe its only your perception? Can you do a consistant 0 to 60 time run anywhere and quantify the difference? When I had a stock 74 superbeetle I use to take it out on one streach of open road and run it WFO, top end was 84mph if everything was tuned correctly. If I couldnt hit 84mph on a calm day on that streach of road I knew it was time for a tuneup.||||reg||1
Z||000005||stsmithgt||04-18-2001||11:39 AM||||Maybe that's what made water injection popular in the early 80's?????||||reg||1
Z||000006||Voytek||04-18-2001||12:06 PM||||Kerri98 - I'd love to post a picture if I knew how to.  By the time I'd get one scanned and posted, this topic will be old news.  But more seriously, someone should start a topic with PFF Members' photos!  I think all of us would be interested in what everyone looks like! (and by then maybe I'll figure out how to post one).<P>Back to the topic, thanks for all the valuable info!  I guess I understand what you're all saying and, to a point, I knew a lot of it.  I guess my real question is if your Fieros are just as succeptable to the weather changes?  I mean the difference is QUITE noticeable.  Calgary is a pretty dry place most of the time (20 - 50% humidity) and occasionally it hits 60-80%.  I have never before driven or owned a car this succeptable to humidity changes.  One day I feel like I'm driving a Beemer (or at least like I could take one) and next day it feels like a Scoda (and I almost wouldn't attempt racing a Scoda on those days).||||reg||10
